export const satRwQuestions: SatBankQuestion[] = [
  // ===== Central Ideas and Details (20) =====
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'easy', question: "Along the Pacific coast, sea otters eat large numbers of sea urchins. Urchins, in turn, graze on kelp, the tall seaweed that forms underwater forests. Where otters disappeared in the 1800s because of hunting, urchin populations exploded and kelp forests shrank to bare rock. Where otters have returned, the kelp has grown back, sheltering fish and other marine life.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Urchin numbers fell along the Pacific coast when otters were hunted in the 1800s.", "By keeping urchin numbers down, sea otters help kelp forests survive.", "Kelp forests provide shelter for fish and many other marine animals.", "Sea otters returned to the Pacific coast after kelp forests regrew."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The text traces a chain: otters eat urchins, urchins eat kelp, so kelp thrives where otters are present and vanishes where they are gone. Urchin numbers exploded, not fell, when otters were hunted; shelter for fish is a supporting detail; and the text has the kelp recovering because otters returned, not the reverse." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'easy', question: "Monarch butterflies that emerge in late summer differ from those born earlier in the year. Summer monarchs live only two to six weeks, but the late-season generation can survive for up to eight months. This long-lived generation flies thousands of kilometers south to Mexico, where it spends the winter clustered in fir forests before heading north again in spring.\n\nAccording to the text, how does the late-season generation of monarchs differ from earlier generations?", options: ["It lives much longer and migrates south for the winter.", "It hatches down south in Mexico and then flies north to breed.", "It lives two to six weeks and stays in the same area.", "It spends the winter clustered in fir forests up north."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The text says the late-season generation lives up to eight months and flies south to Mexico for the winter. It flies to Mexico rather than hatching there, the two-to-six-week lifespan belongs to summer monarchs, and the fir forests where it winters are in Mexico, not up north." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "For decades, historians assumed that medieval European peasants rarely traveled more than a few miles from home. Recent analyses of court records, pilgrimage logs, and tax rolls tell a different story. Many peasants journeyed to distant markets, shrines, and seasonal jobs, sometimes crossing regional borders several times in a single year. Such mobility, researchers argue, spread goods and ideas far more quickly than earlier models allowed.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Pilgrimage logs are the most reliable source for studying the lives of medieval peasants.", "Tax rolls suggest that medieval peasants traveled mainly to avoid paying taxes.", "Historians have long agreed that peasants spread goods and ideas across Europe quickly.", "New evidence suggests medieval peasants traveled far more than historians once believed."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text sets an old assumption (peasants stayed home) against new evidence (they traveled widely). Pilgrimage logs are only one of several sources, the text never gives tax avoidance as a motive, and historians previously believed the opposite of rapid spread." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Why do some desert plants open their pores only at night? Most plants take in carbon dioxide through pores that stay open during the day, but open pores also let water escape. Plants that use a process called CAM photosynthesis collect carbon dioxide in the cool night air, store it as an acid, and use it for photosynthesis the next day with their pores sealed. This timing lets them survive in places where daytime water loss would be deadly.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Most plants lose too much water during the day to survive for long in hot deserts.", "CAM plants take in more carbon dioxide than plants that keep their pores open by day.", "Opening their pores at night lets CAM plants photosynthesize and save scarce water.", "Desert plants open their pores at night since the air is too cool for photosynthesis."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The text answers its opening question: collecting carbon dioxide at night and photosynthesizing with sealed pores by day conserves water. Daytime water loss in most plants is background rather than the main point, the text never compares how much carbon dioxide the plants absorb, and the reason for nighttime opening is water loss, not temperature." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The following text is from a short story. Every morning, Ellen checked the mailbox at the end of the lane, though no letter had come from her brother in two years. She told the neighbors she was only stretching her legs. Still, she kept his old room swept, and on his birthday she set an extra plate at the table, then quietly put it away before supper ended.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Ellen has stopped expecting any news from her brother after two long years.", "Ellen's neighbors suspect that she is worried about her missing brother.", "Ellen continues to hope for her brother's return while playing down that hope.", "Ellen hides her worry from the neighbors while privately giving up hope."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Checking the mail daily, sweeping his room, and setting his plate show ongoing hope, while the excuse to the neighbors and the quietly removed plate show her downplaying it. Nothing indicates she has given up or what the neighbors suspect, and she hides her hope from the neighbors rather than her worry." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Architect Diébédo Francis Kéré designed the Gando Primary School in Burkina Faso using compressed clay bricks made by local residents. A wide metal roof, raised above the classrooms on a steel frame, lets hot air escape and shades the building from the sun. The design keeps classrooms cool without air conditioning, and because villagers learned to make the bricks, they could maintain and expand the school themselves.\n\nAccording to the text, why was it significant that villagers made the bricks?", options: ["It kept the school's classrooms cool without air conditioning.", "It allowed the school's roof to be raised above the classrooms.", "It reduced the cost of the steel used to maintain the frame.", "It let the community maintain the school without outside help."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The last sentence states that because villagers learned to make the bricks, they could maintain and expand the school. Cooling comes from the raised roof, and the text says nothing about brickmaking affecting the roof's frame or the cost of steel." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water bears, can survive being dried out for years. As they lose water, they produce proteins that form a glass-like gel inside their cells, holding delicate structures in place. When water returns, the gel dissolves and the animals resume normal activity. Researchers hope that studying these proteins could lead to better ways of storing vaccines without refrigeration.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Tardigrades survive drying out by means of proteins that may have practical uses.", "Tardigrades can survive for years because they rarely need water to stay active.", "Tardigrade research may already let vaccines be stored without refrigeration.", "Tardigrade proteins form a gel that stays in their cells even after water returns."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The text explains how special proteins protect dried-out tardigrades and notes that researchers hope to apply them. Tardigrades do need water to be active; vaccine storage is only a hope, not an achievement; and the gel dissolves once water returns." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The bristlecone pines of California's White Mountains grow in thin, rocky soil where little else survives. Their growth is so slow that a trunk may widen by less than a millimeter in a year. Dense wood resists insects and rot, and branches that die back reduce the tree's needs during droughts. These harsh conditions, rather than threatening the trees, are the very reason some have lived for more than 4,000 years.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Bristlecone pines grow slowly because insects damage their dense wood.", "The harsh conditions bristlecone pines face contribute to their long lives.", "Bristlecone pines are threatened by the harsh, dry conditions of their habitat.", "The thin soil of the White Mountains supports a wide variety of trees."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The final sentence states the central claim: harsh conditions are the reason the trees live so long, and the earlier details show how. The dense wood resists insects, the text says the conditions do not threaten the trees, and it says little else survives in that soil." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In a study of 80 preschoolers, a research team had children sort cards by color and then, without warning, by shape. Children who had spent the previous week playing games that required switching rules made fewer errors after the change than children who had played games with fixed rules. The researchers note that the effect was modest and faded after a month, but they suggest that flexible thinking in young children may respond to targeted practice.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Card-sorting tasks may be the best way to measure flexible thinking in children.", "Practice with rule-switching games may modestly improve children's flexible thinking.", "Games with fixed rules harm preschoolers' ability to adjust to new instructions.", "Preschoolers who practice switching rules keep a modest advantage for many months."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The study found a modest, temporary benefit from rule-switching games, and the researchers suggest flexible thinking may respond to practice. The text never ranks measurement methods, shows no harm from fixed-rule games (only a smaller benefit), and says the effect faded after a month." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Some economists argue that raising the price of parking downtown drives shoppers to suburban malls. Others counter that cheap parking encourages drivers to occupy spaces all day, leaving few open for customers. Research by urban planning scholar Donald Shoup supports the second view: when cities have priced curb parking so that about one space per block stays open, nearby businesses have often seen more customer turnover, not less.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Pricing parking so some spaces stay open can benefit downtown businesses.", "Raising parking prices downtown tends to push shoppers toward suburban malls.", "Economists remain evenly divided on how parking prices affect businesses.", "Cities should make curb spaces free so that more shoppers can visit stores."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The text presents two views and then says the evidence supports the second: well-priced parking keeps spaces open and brings more customers. The first view is the one the evidence undercuts, the text does not leave the question evenly split, and free parking is the cause of the problem described." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "In 1896, chemist Svante Arrhenius calculated that doubling the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere would raise Earth's average temperature by several degrees. Working by hand for months, he estimated how much heat the gas absorbs at different latitudes and seasons. Although his numbers were rough, his basic conclusion has been confirmed by modern climate models.\n\nAccording to the text, what did Arrhenius conclude?", options: ["Modern climate models would be needed to measure warming.", "Carbon dioxide absorbs the same heat at every latitude.", "Doubling carbon dioxide would leave temperatures unchanged.", "More carbon dioxide in the air would make the planet warmer."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The first sentence states his calculation: doubling carbon dioxide would raise Earth's average temperature. Modern models later confirmed his result but were not part of his conclusion, he estimated absorption separately for different latitudes, and he predicted warming, not no change." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The Hawaiian honeycreepers, a group of small forest birds, descend from a single species of finch that reached the islands millions of years ago. Over time, the group diversified into more than 50 species with beaks suited to different foods: some sip nectar from curved flowers, some crack hard seeds, and others probe bark for insects. Many of these species are now extinct, and several survivors are found only at high elevations.\n\nAccording to the text, what explains the variety of beak shapes among Hawaiian honeycreepers?", options: ["Several species now survive mainly at high elevations.", "Multiple finch species reached the islands separately.", "Each species adapted to its own main source of food.", "Many species died out, leaving only certain beak types."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The text says the group diversified into species with beaks suited to different foods such as nectar, seeds, and insects. The honeycreepers descend from a single finch species, and the text links neither elevation nor extinction to beak shape." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Urban tree planting is often promoted as a way to cool cities during heat waves, and measurements confirm that shaded streets can be several degrees cooler than exposed ones. A recent study of 20 cities, however, found that the benefit depends heavily on species and placement: trees planted in narrow streets sometimes trapped heat at night, and drought-stressed trees cooled far less than healthy ones.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Planting trees in narrow streets is the most effective way to cool cities.", "Trees can cool cities, but how much varies with the tree and its location.", "Narrow streets gain the most from trees, but trees do little once a heat wave begins.", "Shaded streets are cooler than exposed ones because trees release water at night."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The text confirms that trees cool streets but qualifies that claim: species and placement matter. Trees in narrow streets sometimes trapped heat, the text does not say trees fail during heat waves, and it never explains cooling as nighttime water release." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The following text is from a short story. When the old bakery on Fulton Street closed, Marisol expected to feel relieved; she had complained about the smell of burnt sugar drifting through her window for eleven years. Instead, the first quiet morning felt wrong. She found herself standing at the window at five o'clock, waiting for a light that did not come on.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Marisol unexpectedly misses a routine she used to find annoying.", "Marisol is relieved that she no longer smells burnt sugar each day.", "Marisol regrets rarely visiting the bakery while it was open.", "Marisol hopes to buy the bakery building and reopen it herself."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "She expected relief after years of complaints, but the quiet morning 'felt wrong' and she waits at the window for the bakery's light. The text says she did not feel the relief she expected, and it mentions neither a visit to the bakery nor any plan to reopen it." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "It is often said that humans use only 10 percent of their brains. Brain-imaging studies contradict this claim. Over the course of a day, virtually every region of the brain shows activity, and even simple tasks such as reading a sentence engage areas spread across both hemispheres. Moreover, damage to almost any part of the brain produces some loss of function, which would be unlikely if most of the organ sat idle.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Reading engages more brain regions than most other everyday tasks do.", "Brain damage causes loss of function only in the brain's busiest areas.", "Brain-imaging studies have not yet tested the claim about 10 percent use.", "Research indicates that the popular belief about brain use is mistaken."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text states a common belief and then presents imaging and brain-damage evidence against it. It never compares reading with other tasks, says damage to almost any part causes some loss, and reports that imaging studies contradict the claim." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The Inca built no wheeled carts and kept no written records, yet they governed an empire stretching more than 4,000 kilometers along the Andes. Relay runners carried messages along a road network of some 40,000 kilometers, covering hundreds of kilometers a day. Officials recorded census counts and tribute on khipus, cords whose knots encoded numbers. Storehouses along the roads held food and cloth for soldiers and travelers.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["The Inca relied mainly on soldiers to carry messages across their road network.", "Khipus were used chiefly to record the histories and family lines of Inca rulers.", "The Inca used effective alternatives to wheels and writing to run their empire.", "The Inca empire eventually declined because it lacked wheels and written records."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The first sentence sets up the puzzle (no wheels or writing, yet a vast empire) and the rest shows how runners, khipus, and storehouses filled the gap. Messages went by relay runners, khipus recorded census counts and tribute, and the text does not discuss the empire's decline." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Octopuses can change the color and texture of their skin in a fraction of a second. Specialized cells called chromatophores, each containing a sac of pigment, expand or shrink when tiny muscles around them contract or relax. Beneath these cells, other layers reflect light to produce shimmering blues and greens. Because the muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the changes happen far faster than in animals that rely on hormones.\n\nAccording to the text, why can octopuses change color so quickly?", options: ["Their nerves release hormones that alter the pigment.", "Their reflective cells produce blue and green light.", "Their pigment cells are controlled directly by nerves.", "Hormones directly control the size of their pigment sacs."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The last sentence gives the reason: the muscles around the pigment cells are controlled directly by nerves. The text contrasts this with the slower hormone control of other animals, so neither hormones released by nerves nor hormone control of the sacs fits, and the reflective layers explain certain colors rather than speed." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Many early photographers in the 1840s saw the camera as a purely scientific instrument. Anna Atkins, a British botanist, used the cyanotype process to record specimens of algae, placing them directly on light-sensitive paper. Her 1843 book of these images is often called the first book illustrated with photographic images. Yet Atkins arranged each specimen with evident care for composition, and art historians now study her plates as works of art as well as scientific records.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Atkins was the first scientist to treat the camera mainly as an artistic tool.", "Atkins's botanical images are scientific records that also have artistic value.", "Atkins's botanical records of algae were more accurate than drawings of the period.", "Atkins invented the cyanotype process to create the images in her 1843 book."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The text's turn ('Yet...') shows that Atkins's scientific images are also valued as art. It never calls her the first to treat photography as art (she did not even use a camera), never compares her images with drawings, and says she used the cyanotype process, not that she invented it." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Chien-Shiung Wu was already a respected physicist when, in 1956, two colleagues proposed that a basic law of physics, the conservation of parity, might fail in certain radioactive decays. Wu designed the experiment to test the idea, cooling cobalt-60 atoms to near absolute zero and measuring the direction of emitted electrons. Her results showed that parity was not conserved, overturning an assumption physicists had held for decades.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Wu's work disproved a principle long accepted in physics.", "Wu first proposed that parity might not be conserved.", "Wu became respected in physics because of her 1956 work.", "Wu showed that cobalt-60 atoms are hard to cool down."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The text focuses on Wu's experiment and its result: parity is not conserved, contrary to a decades-old assumption. Two colleagues proposed the idea, she was already respected before 1956, and cooling the atoms was part of her method, not her finding." },
  { skillSlug: S.central, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Coral reefs cover less than 1 percent of the ocean floor, yet they support roughly a quarter of all known marine species. Their complex structures offer shelter and breeding sites, and the algae living inside coral tissues supply energy that fuels the reef's food web. When water temperatures rise too high, corals expel these algae, and prolonged bleaching can leave the reef without its main energy source.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", options: ["Reefs cover little of the ocean floor, but they support more marine species than any other habitat.", "The algae inside coral tissues supply the shelter and breeding sites that reef species use.", "Bleaching happens when corals expel their algae because water temperatures fall too low.", "Reefs support many marine species, but high temperatures can deprive them of nourishment."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text establishes reefs' importance to marine life and then explains how heat-driven bleaching can cut off their energy source. It says reefs support about a quarter of species, not more than any habitat; shelter comes from the reef structure, not the algae; and bleaching follows high, not low, temperatures." },

  // ===== Inferences (26) =====
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'easy', question: "The plant Mimosa pudica folds its leaves when touched. In one experiment, researchers repeatedly dropped potted mimosas a short distance onto a padded surface. At first, the plants folded their leaves after each drop, but after many drops they stopped responding, even though they still folded their leaves when shaken in a new way. This suggests that the plants ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["had lost the ability to fold their leaves in response to touch.", "had become used to being dropped rather than simply worn out.", "were damaged by being dropped onto the padded surface.", "fold their leaves only when they are dropped from a height."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Because the plants still folded their leaves when shaken in a new way, they had not lost the ability; they stopped reacting only to the repeated, harmless drops. Nothing indicates damage, and the plants folded their leaves in response to touch and shaking, not only drops." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'easy', question: "Archaeologists have found that potters at a site in Mesopotamia began using a fast-spinning wheel around 3500 BCE; earlier pots there were built by hand from coils of clay. A shard found in a sealed layer at the site shows the smooth, even ridges produced only by a fast wheel. The layer containing the shard therefore most likely ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["formed no sooner than about 3500 BCE.", "formed well before 3500 BCE.", "contains only pots built from coils.", "was disturbed by later digging."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Only a fast wheel makes those ridges, and fast wheels appeared around 3500 BCE, so the layer cannot be much older than that. Coil-built pots were the earlier method, and the layer is described as sealed, which argues against later disturbance." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Linguists studying the Pirahã language of Brazil report that it has no words for exact numbers, only terms meaning roughly 'few' and 'many.' In matching tasks, Pirahã speakers reliably lined up small sets of objects, but their accuracy dropped sharply for sets larger than three. These results suggest that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["the Pirahã have words for numbers that researchers did not record.", "people everywhere may struggle to match sets of more than three objects.", "precise counting of big quantities may rely on words for numbers.", "Pirahã speakers have no interest in counting objects in daily life."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Speakers without exact number words did well with small sets but poorly with larger ones, which points to number words as a tool for exact counting. The text says the language lacks such words, the study involved only Pirahã speakers, and it says nothing about their interests." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Wood frogs survive winter in Alaska by allowing much of the water in their bodies to freeze. Before freezing, their livers flood the blood with glucose, which keeps ice from forming inside cells, where it would tear cell structures apart. In a lab test, frogs that were prevented from producing extra glucose did not recover after thawing. Glucose, therefore, ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["causes the water in the frogs' bodies to freeze more quickly.", "is produced by the frogs only after the ice begins to melt.", "appears to help the frogs' livers survive but not the rest of their cells.", "appears essential to the frogs' ability to survive freezing."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Frogs without extra glucose did not survive thawing, so glucose appears necessary for surviving freezing. The text says glucose prevents ice inside cells rather than speeding freezing, it is released before freezing, and it protects cells generally." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Most bats hunt by echolocation, emitting calls and listening for the echoes that bounce off prey. The fringe-lipped bat of Central America can echolocate too, but it also eavesdrops on the mating calls of male túngara frogs to find its meals. Male frogs that add extra notes, called chucks, to their calls attract more females. The same chucks, however, ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["are too faint for the female frogs to hear over long distances.", "help the males avoid bats by masking the rest of the call.", "also make it easier for the bats to locate the calling males.", "are produced only by frogs that live far from bat habitats."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "'However' signals a cost to set against the benefit of attracting females; since the bats hunt by listening to frog calls, extra notes would also draw bats. The text says chucks attract females, so they are not too faint, and nothing suggests they hide the call or depend on location." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Some stone tools found in Kenya are about 3.3 million years old, older than the earliest known fossils of the genus Homo. Some researchers therefore propose that an earlier human relative, such as Australopithecus, made them. However, no fossils have been found alongside the tools, so it remains possible that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["researchers have greatly overestimated the age of the tools.", "the tools were made by a species that is not yet identified.", "Australopithecus made the tools found at other sites as well.", "the genus Homo lived only in Kenya at that time."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Without fossils found with the tools, the toolmaker is uncertain, so it could be a species not yet known. The missing fossils do not cast doubt on the tools' age, other sites are never discussed, and nothing supports Homo living only in Kenya." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A survey of 2,000 adults found that people who reported reading fiction regularly scored higher, on average, on a test of recognizing emotions in others' facial expressions. Because the survey measured both traits at one point in time, it cannot show whether reading fiction builds this skill or whether people who already have the skill ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["stop reading fiction as they grow older.", "score lower on tests of facial expressions.", "read nonfiction more often than fiction.", "are more likely to choose to read fiction."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The sentence sets up the two possible directions of a correlation: reading builds the skill, or people with the skill tend to become fiction readers. The other completions do not offer an alternative explanation for why the two traits go together." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "In the Atacama Desert of Chile, some weather stations have recorded almost no rain for decades. Yet a band of lichens and cacti thrives on the coastal hills. Each winter, a dense fog called camanchaca rolls in from the Pacific, and droplets collect on plant surfaces. Plants on the coastal hills can thus ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["draw water from fog despite the lack of rain.", "survive only in years with unusually heavy rain.", "grow in most parts of the desert beyond the coast.", "store enough rainwater to last many decades."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The fog supplies droplets where rain almost never falls, which explains how the plants thrive. Rain is nearly absent for decades, the plants are described only on the coastal hills, and there is no rainwater to store." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "A compound called fisetin extended the lifespan of mice by about 10 percent in one study, apparently by clearing out aging cells that release inflammatory signals. The compound is sold as a supplement, and some advertisements now describe it as an anti-aging treatment for people. However, clinical trials in humans are still underway, so claims about its benefits for people ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["have been disproved by the results in mice.", "rest on human trials that have already finished.", "go beyond what the current evidence has established.", "are weaker than the claims about its safety."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The evidence so far comes from mice, and human trials are unfinished, so claims about people outrun the evidence. The mouse results were positive, the text says the human trials are still underway, and safety claims are never discussed." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "When the town of Alder Falls replaced its weekly trash pickup with pickup every other week, its collection costs fell by nearly a third. The change also led to more complaints about overflowing bins, and a survey found that residents' satisfaction with town services dropped. For other towns considering a similar change, Alder Falls's experience suggests that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["less frequent pickup has no effect on how residents view services.", "saving money may come at the cost of residents' satisfaction.", "reducing pickup may raise the town's collection costs over time.", "residents' satisfaction will rise once they see the savings."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Costs fell but satisfaction dropped, so the lesson is a tradeoff between savings and residents' approval. Satisfaction did change, costs fell rather than rose, and nothing suggests satisfaction will rebound once residents notice the savings." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Mountain gorillas in Rwanda build a new nest of branches and leaves every night rather than reusing an old one. Droppings left in a used nest can harbor the eggs of intestinal parasites, and among other primates, groups that reuse sleeping sites tend to carry more parasites than groups that change sites often. Building fresh nests may therefore help gorillas ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["spread parasites to other groups.", "stay warm on cold mountain nights.", "hide their location from predators.", "limit their exposure to parasites."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Used nests can harbor parasite eggs, and primates that reuse sleeping sites carry more parasites, so fresh nests likely reduce the gorillas' own parasite exposure. Nothing suggests fresh nests spread parasites to others, and the text mentions nothing about warmth or predators." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In a study of 1,500 neighborhoods, researchers found that areas with more public libraries per resident also had higher average reading scores among children. The researchers cautioned against concluding that libraries raise scores, noting that neighborhoods with more libraries also tended to have higher household incomes. The findings are therefore consistent with the possibility that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["income affects both library access and children's reading scores.", "libraries and reading scores are unrelated in every neighborhood.", "children in higher-income areas visit libraries less often than others.", "adding libraries to a neighborhood would sharply raise reading scores."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The researchers point to income as a third factor that could explain why libraries and scores go together. The data show that libraries and scores are related, so calling them unrelated contradicts the findings; library visits are never measured; and the caution is precisely against concluding that libraries raise scores." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A carved bone flute found in a German cave has been dated to about 40,000 years ago. Stone tools in the same layer match those made by early modern humans rather than those made by Neanderthals, who also lived in the region. If the dating and the tool identification are both correct, the flute was most likely ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["made by early Neanderthals.", "carved by modern humans much later.", "brought from another region.", "made by early modern humans."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The flute lies in the same layer as tools made by early modern humans, so they most likely made it too. The tools point away from Neanderthals, sharing a layer dated to about 40,000 years ago means the flute was not carved much later, and nothing suggests it came from elsewhere." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "The following text is from a short story. For weeks, Tomás had insisted that the old truck needed only a new belt. When the mechanic listed the other failing parts, Tomás studied the grease-stained floor for a long moment before saying, 'Fine. Maybe it's more than the belt.' He did not look at his sister, who had said the same thing a month earlier. The text suggests that Tomás ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["admits he was wrong but is reluctant to acknowledge it openly.", "eagerly agrees with the mechanic's view of the truck's problems.", "still believes that the truck needs nothing but a new belt.", "is angry with the mechanic for listing unnecessary repairs."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "He concedes ('Maybe it's more than the belt') but stares at the floor and avoids his sister, who had been right, showing reluctance. His pause and grudging 'Fine' are not eager, his words show he no longer insists on the belt alone, and nothing suggests anger at the mechanic." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "In one study, bumblebee colonies were fed either sunflower pollen or a mixture of wildflower pollen. Colonies fed sunflower pollen had markedly fewer infections from a common gut parasite. Because the colonies were otherwise kept under identical conditions, the researchers concluded that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["wildflower pollen causes the gut parasite to infect the bumblebees.", "sunflower pollen probably lowered the colonies' infection rates.", "sunflowers are the only plants that can protect bee colonies.", "bumblebees probably prefer the taste of sunflower pollen to wildflowers."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "With all other conditions identical, the difference in infections points to the sunflower pollen. The study does not show that wildflower pollen causes infection, tested only two diets rather than all plants, and did not measure preference." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "A study of 400 households in Phoenix found that installing smart thermostats reduced summer energy use by an average of 12 percent. The study's authors note, however, that Phoenix summers are unusually long and hot, and that most homes in the sample were built after 2000. It would therefore be premature to conclude that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["installing smart thermostats cut energy use in the Phoenix homes studied.", "Phoenix summers are longer and hotter than those in most places.", "smart thermostats would save as much energy in homes everywhere.", "most of the homes in the study sample were built after 2000."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The unusual climate and newer homes mean the Phoenix result may not generalize, so a claim about homes everywhere is premature. The reduction in the studied homes is the study's finding, the sample's construction dates are stated directly, and the authors themselves describe Phoenix summers as unusually long and hot." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Many harmless insects have evolved to resemble species that predators avoid, a strategy called Batesian mimicry. Birds that have been stung by a wasp learn to avoid black-and-yellow insects, which benefits any insect with that pattern. The hoverfly, for example, ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["has a painful sting but lacks a wasp's yellow and black stripes.", "is avoided by birds because it tastes worse than most wasps.", "hunts wasps and other stinging insects for most of its food.", "lacks a sting but has a wasp's yellow and black stripes."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "'For example' calls for a harmless insect that resembles a dangerous one; a stingless fly with wasp-like stripes fits. A stinging or foul-tasting hoverfly would not be harmless, and its diet is irrelevant to mimicry." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Trials in several countries have shown that no-till farming, which leaves crop residue on fields instead of plowing it under, reduces soil erosion and often maintains yields. Yet in many regions the practice remains rare. Switching requires new planting equipment, and yields can dip during the first few years while soil adjusts. This suggests that farmers' decisions depend on ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["whether the trials were conducted in their own countries.", "evidence that no-till farming reduces soil erosion.", "short-term costs, not just long-run benefits to soil.", "the size of the harvest they expect in the long run."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The practice has proven benefits yet stays rare, and the text points to upfront equipment costs and early yield dips, so near-term costs weigh on decisions. Where trials took place is never linked to adoption, the erosion evidence already exists, and long-run yields are often maintained." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The ancient city of Petra, in present-day Jordan, received very little rain, yet it supported a population of perhaps 20,000 people. Its builders carved channels into the sandstone cliffs to direct runoff from rare storms into cisterns and dams. Archaeologists have found that some of these systems could store millions of liters. Petra's water system thus allowed the city to ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["house a sizable community despite scarce rainfall.", "trade water with cities that received more rain.", "avoid flooding during its frequent heavy storms.", "grow its population faster than other cities nearby."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Capturing and storing rare storm runoff let a city of about 20,000 survive with little rain. The text mentions no water trade, storms were rare rather than frequent, and it never compares Petra's growth with other cities'." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In a field trial, agronomist Dana Whitfield applied a new microbial fertilizer to wheat plots in three regions. Yields rose by about 15 percent in plots with sandy, low-nitrogen soil, but in plots with rich clay soil the fertilizer made no measurable difference. Whitfield concludes that farmers should not expect the product to ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["increase wheat yields in plots with sandy, low-nitrogen soil.", "improve harvests to the same degree on every type of soil.", "work in regions outside the three included in her trial.", "cost less than the fertilizers that farmers currently use."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The fertilizer helped on sandy soil but not on clay, so its benefit depends on soil type. It did raise yields on sandy soil, the trial's regions are not the issue, and cost is never discussed." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The painter Artemisia Gentileschi signed only some of her works, and for centuries several of her paintings were attributed to her father, Orazio, or to other artists. Recent technical studies have compared brushwork and pigments in disputed paintings with those in works she is known to have signed. As a result, several paintings have been reassigned to her, which suggests that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["Orazio copied his daughter's style in his own paintings.", "her body of work is larger than was once recognized.", "she stopped signing her paintings later in her career.", "most paintings from her era have been attributed wrongly."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Paintings once credited to others are now credited to her, so her known output has grown. Nothing indicates Orazio copied her, when she signed works is not discussed, and the text concerns her paintings, not most paintings of the era." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Perovskite solar cells can convert sunlight into electricity about as efficiently as standard silicon cells and are cheaper to manufacture. However, perovskite cells tend to break down within a few years when exposed to heat and moisture, while silicon panels commonly last 25 years or more. Until the durability problem is solved, perovskite cells are most likely to be adopted for uses in which ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["the panels must survive decades of outdoor wind and weather.", "high efficiency is more important than a low price.", "a low upfront cost matters more than a long working life.", "panels will face constant heat and heavy moisture."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Perovskite cells are cheaper but short-lived, so they suit uses where price outweighs longevity. Decades outdoors or constant heat and moisture are exactly the conditions they handle poorly, and their efficiency is only comparable to silicon's, so it is not their advantage." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter, when temperatures can fall below −40°C. The males incubate the eggs for about two months without eating, huddling in tightly packed groups that slowly rotate so each bird takes turns at the wind-exposed edge. Researchers have found that birds at the center of a huddle can be much warmer than the surrounding air, which suggests that huddling helps males ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["find food for their mates during the coldest winter months.", "avoid ever spending time at the edge of the group.", "incubate their eggs faster than other penguins do.", "conserve the energy they need to survive the long fast."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Males go two months without food in extreme cold, and huddling keeps them warm, which saves energy. They do not eat or hunt during this period, the rotating huddle means each bird takes turns at the edge, and incubation speed is never compared." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In a preliminary study, 30 people with mild insomnia who listened to recorded pink noise at bedtime reported falling asleep faster than they had the week before. The study had no comparison group, and participants knew the purpose of the recordings. Taken together, these details suggest that the results ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["may reflect participants' expectations rather than the noise itself.", "prove that pink noise reliably shortens the time needed to fall asleep.", "may show that pink noise is ineffective for people with severe insomnia.", "came from comparing participants with a similar group of non-listeners."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "With no comparison group and participants aware of the purpose, their expectations could explain the improvement. A preliminary, uncontrolled study cannot prove the effect, people with severe insomnia were not studied, and the text states there was no comparison group." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The Great Library of Alexandria is often said to have been destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. Historians point out, however, that ancient sources describe several damaging events over hundreds of years, and that the library's funding and scholarly staff declined long before its final disappearance. The library's loss is therefore better understood as ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["a gradual decline than as one sudden event.", "one sudden event than as a gradual process.", "a myth, since the library never truly existed.", "a decline in funding caused by one large fire."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Several damaging events and a long decline in funding and staff point to a gradual loss rather than one fire. The single-fire account is what historians question, the library's existence is not in doubt, and the funding decline came before, not from, any fire." },
  { skillSlug: S.infer, difficulty: 'hard', question: "After the town of Port Ellery banned plastic shopping bags in 2019, the number of plastic bags found during annual beach cleanups fell by 40 percent. The same year, however, a new current pattern began carrying less debris toward the town's beaches, and nearby towns without bans saw similar declines. These observations suggest that ______\n\nWhich choice most logically completes the text?", options: ["the decline in bags on beaches was caused mostly by the ban.", "towns without bans may have produced more plastic waste than Port Ellery.", "beach cleanups became less thorough in the years after the ban.", "the ban may not be the main cause of the drop in bags on beaches."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "A shift in currents and similar drops in towns without bans offer other explanations, so the ban may not be the main cause. That undercuts rather than supports crediting the ban, waste production is never compared, and cleanup thoroughness is not mentioned." },

  // ===== Command of Evidence (8) =====
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Ecologist Mei Tanaka hypothesizes that crows in some cities have learned to use traffic to crack walnuts: the birds drop the nuts onto crosswalks so that passing cars break the shells, then retrieve the pieces when cars stop at red lights.\n\nWhich finding, if true, would most directly support Tanaka's hypothesis?", options: ["Crows in rural areas drop walnuts onto rocks while perched high above the ground.", "Crows drop nuts while cars are moving and collect them when the signal turns red.", "Walnut trees are more common along city streets than in the nearby countryside.", "City crows eat a wider variety of foods than crows that live in forests do."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The hypothesis has two parts, using cars to crack nuts and using red lights to collect them safely, and only the timing of dropping and collecting matches both. Rural crows using rocks, the location of walnut trees, and city crows' varied diets say nothing about crows using traffic." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A student hypothesizes that dragonflies at a local pond are more active on warmer days. She counted dragonflies on five mornings and recorded the temperature each time: 14°C, 6 dragonflies; 18°C, 11 dragonflies; 21°C, 17 dragonflies; 24°C, 23 dragonflies; and 27°C, 30 dragonflies.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses the data to support the student's hypothesis?", options: ["The student counted 30 dragonflies on the warmest of the five mornings.", "The student counted a total of 87 dragonflies across all five mornings.", "Counts rose steadily from 6 at 14°C to 30 at 27°C as mornings grew warmer.", "The coolest morning, at 14°C, had the fewest dragonflies, with 6 of them."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The hypothesis links activity to temperature, so the support must show counts rising as temperatures rise across the mornings. A single warm or cool morning shows no trend, and the five-day total (6 + 11 + 17 + 23 + 30 = 87) ignores temperature entirely." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Researchers compared two methods of teaching fractions. Students taught with number lines improved their test scores by an average of 18 points, while students taught with pie diagrams improved by an average of 11 points. The number-line group also spent more class time on the unit. A school administrator claims that the number-line students improved more than the pie-diagram students did.\n\nWhich choice best describes data from the study that support the administrator's claim?", options: ["The number-line group spent more class time on the fractions unit overall.", "Pie-diagram students improved their test scores by an average of 11 points.", "Number-line students improved by 7 more points than pie-diagram students.", "Students in both groups improved their test scores after the fraction unit."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The claim compares the two groups' improvement, and 18 − 11 = 7 points is that comparison. Class time is accurate but does not bear on the size of the improvement, one group's gain alone cannot show which improved more, and 'both improved' makes no comparison." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A plant biologist claims that a new spray-on coating helps tomato seedlings survive frost. To test the claim, she exposed 200 coated seedlings and 200 uncoated seedlings to the same overnight frost.\n\nWhich result, if true, would most strongly support the biologist's claim?", options: ["Most of the coated seedlings were still alive a week after the frost.", "Many more coated than uncoated seedlings were alive a week later.", "The uncoated seedlings grew taller than the coated ones before the frost.", "Both groups lost about the same number of seedlings to the frost."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "To show the coating helps, coated seedlings must survive better than uncoated ones under the same frost. Survival of the coated group alone has no comparison, height before the frost is irrelevant, and equal losses would weaken the claim." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Economist Ravi Patel claims that the share of workers in one country who work remotely jumped in 2020 and has not returned to its earlier level. A national survey reports the share of workers who worked remotely at least one day per week: 2016, 9%; 2018, 12%; 2020, 31%; 2022, 27%; and 2024, 29%.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses data from the survey to support Patel's claim?", options: ["The share of workers who worked remotely was 9% in 2016 and 12% in 2018.", "The share of workers who worked remotely fell from 31% in 2020 to 27% in 2022.", "The share of workers who worked remotely was higher in 2024 than in 2022.", "The share rose from 12% in 2018 to 31% in 2020 and was still 29% in 2024."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Patel's claim has two parts: a jump in 2020 and no return to the earlier level. The rise from 12% to 31% shows the jump, and 29% in 2024 shows the share stayed far above 12%. The other choices each cover only part of the period: 2016 to 2018 shows neither the jump nor what followed, the 2020 to 2022 drop alone suggests a return toward the earlier level, and comparing 2024 with 2022 says nothing about the pre-2020 level." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Historian Lena Ortiz has studied hundreds of letters written by workers at a 19th-century New England textile mill. She argues that the workers valued the independence their wages gave them, even while they criticized the mill's long working hours.\n\nWhich finding about the letters, if true, would most directly support Ortiz's argument?", options: ["Workers often praised earning their own money while objecting to 13-hour shifts.", "Workers often described the machinery of the mill in careful technical detail.", "Workers often complained about long shifts while urging others not to take mill jobs.", "Workers often wrote to relatives on farms asking for news about the harvest."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Ortiz's argument pairs two attitudes, valuing wages and independence while criticizing long hours, and only letters showing both support it. Letters urging others to avoid mill jobs show criticism without the valued independence, and descriptions of machinery or requests for farm news bear on neither part." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'hard', question: "A researcher claims that listening to music while studying has no meaningful effect on how well students remember vocabulary. In an experiment, one group studied 40 words in silence and another studied the same words while listening to music; both groups were tested the next day.\n\nWhich result, if true, would most directly support the researcher's claim?", options: ["The group that studied in silence recalled far more words.", "The two groups recalled nearly the same average number of words.", "Students in both groups said they preferred to study with music.", "Students who studied with music finished studying more quickly."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A claim of no effect is supported when the groups perform about the same. A large advantage for the silent group would contradict it, and students' preferences or study speed do not measure how well they remembered the words." },
  { skillSlug: S.evidence, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A student claims that a town's recycling program has been more successful in its newer neighborhoods. Town data show the percentage of households that recycle in four neighborhoods: Oak Hill (built in the 1950s), 38%; Riverside (1970s), 44%; Maple Park (2005), 71%; and Harbor View (2015), 76%.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses the data to support the student's claim?", options: ["The rate in Riverside is 6 percentage points higher than the rate in Oak Hill.", "Maple Park, which was built in 2005, has a household recycling rate of 71 percent.", "The two neighborhoods built most recently have the two highest recycling rates.", "Oak Hill, the oldest neighborhood in the town, has a recycling rate of only 38 percent."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The claim compares newer and older neighborhoods, and only the statement that the two newest have the two highest rates makes that comparison. The Riverside and Oak Hill comparison involves only older areas, and a single neighborhood's rate cannot show a pattern by age." },

  // ===== Words in Context (10) =====
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Although the documentary covers two centuries of history in under an hour, its director avoids oversimplifying. Each segment is ______, focusing closely on a single decisive event and the people involved rather than attempting to survey an entire era.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", options: ["exhaustive", "chronological", "improvised", "selective"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Focusing on a single event rather than surveying an entire era means each segment is selective. 'Exhaustive' is the opposite, 'chronological' describes order rather than focus, and nothing suggests the segments were unplanned." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Marine biologist Sylvia Earle has spent more than 7,000 hours underwater, and her ______ commitment to ocean research has lasted for over six decades, from her early dives in the 1950s to her campaigns for protected marine areas today.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", options: ["enduring", "fleeting", "reluctant", "theoretical"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "A commitment lasting over six decades is enduring. 'Fleeting' contradicts that span, 'reluctant' conflicts with thousands of hours of dives, and her hands-on work is not merely theoretical." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "When the first images from the new telescope were released, even veteran astronomers were surprised. The pictures revealed thousands of faint galaxies in a patch of sky that had appeared nearly empty, a result so ______ that it made headlines around the world.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", options: ["predictable", "forceful", "tentative", "remarkable"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "A result that surprised experts and made headlines worldwide is remarkable. It was not predictable, 'forceful' describes physical force rather than something notable, and nothing marks the result as tentative." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Early reviewers called the novel a masterpiece. Critic Joan Ellis was more cautious: she praised its vivid characters but qualified her enthusiasm, noting that the final chapters felt rushed and left several plot threads unresolved.\n\nAs used in the text, what does the word \"qualified\" most nearly mean?", options: ["Certified", "Limited", "Strengthened", "Entitled"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Ellis was 'more cautious' and added criticisms, so she limited her praise. 'Certified' and 'entitled' are other senses of 'qualified' that do not fit an attitude, and 'strengthened' is the opposite of what the criticisms do." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'hard', question: "The idea that dinosaurs were sluggish, cold-blooded reptiles held sway for much of the 20th century. Since the 1970s, however, evidence of fast growth rates and active lifestyles has given the view that many dinosaurs were warm-blooded considerable currency among paleontologists.\n\nAs used in the text, what does the word \"currency\" most nearly mean?", options: ["Monetary value", "Recency", "Acceptance", "Urgency"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "New evidence has made the warm-blooded view widely accepted among paleontologists, the sense of 'currency' used for ideas in general circulation. 'Monetary value' is the money sense, and neither recency nor urgency describes how evidence changed the view's standing." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'hard', question: "The committee rejected the proposal not because its goals were unworthy but because its reasoning was not sound: the budget projections relied on figures from a decade-old survey, and the plan ignored rising construction costs.\n\nAs used in the text, what does the word \"sound\" most nearly mean?", options: ["Valid", "Audible", "Profound", "Intact"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The flaws listed, outdated figures and ignored costs, show that the reasoning was not valid. 'Audible' is the noise sense of 'sound,' 'profound' describes depth rather than correctness, and 'intact' fits objects, not reasoning." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Residents had hoped the new highway would ease traffic, but within a year, commute times had grown longer. Critics described the project as ______, arguing that the city had spent heavily on a solution that made the problem worse.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", options: ["ambitious", "visionary", "misguided", "efficient"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Critics argue the costly project made traffic worse, a negative judgment that 'misguided' captures. 'Ambitious' and 'visionary' are neutral or positive, and 'efficient' contradicts the complaint." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Although the city council has repeatedly rejected plans for a downtown stadium, the council president announced that members would entertain a revised proposal next month if it included a detailed plan for public transit.\n\nAs used in the text, what does the word \"entertain\" most nearly mean?", options: ["Consider", "Amuse", "Host", "Authorize"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The council would be willing to consider a revised proposal, not necessarily authorize it. 'Amuse' and 'host' are other senses of 'entertain' that do not fit a proposal, and authorizing it goes beyond agreeing to review it." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Botanist Ynes Mexia began collecting plants at age 55 and, over the next thirteen years, gathered roughly 145,000 specimens across Mexico and South America. Her ______ fieldwork, which often took her into remote mountains for months at a time, yielded hundreds of species new to science.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", options: ["casual", "tireless", "brief", "hesitant"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Collecting some 145,000 specimens on months-long trips describes tireless work. 'Casual' and 'hesitant' clash with that effort, and 'brief' conflicts with thirteen years of months-long expeditions." },
  { skillSlug: S.words, difficulty: 'hard', question: "For years, scientists attributed the decline of a songbird population to habitat loss. A new study undermines that explanation: the birds' numbers fell just as sharply in protected forests, where habitat remained intact, as in areas that were logged.\n\nAs used in the text, what does the word \"undermines\" most nearly mean?", options: ["Confirms", "Explains", "Excavates", "Weakens"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Declines in protected forests with intact habitat cast doubt on habitat loss as the cause, so the study weakens the explanation. It does not confirm or explain it, and 'excavates' is a literal digging sense that does not fit." },

  // ===== Text Structure and Purpose (19) =====
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Many people picture glaciers as frozen and still, but glaciers flow. Under their own enormous weight, the ice deforms like very thick putty, and meltwater at the base can let a glacier slide over the ground beneath it. Some Greenland glaciers move more than 10 kilometers per year, fast enough that researchers can track their motion from one satellite image to the next.\n\nWhich choice best states the main purpose of the text?", options: ["To argue that satellites are the best tools for studying ice", "To describe how meltwater forms beneath Greenland's glaciers", "To show that glaciers seem to melt even though they rarely move", "To explain how glaciers flow even though they seem stationary"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text corrects the image of still glaciers and explains the mechanisms and speed of their movement. Satellites appear only as a way to observe speed, meltwater is mentioned but its formation is not described, and the text says glaciers move, not that they rarely move." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "For years, the Dutch town of Drachten struggled with traffic accidents at its busy intersections. In 2003, planners tried an unusual approach: they removed most traffic lights and signs, forcing drivers, cyclists, and pedestrians to make eye contact and negotiate each crossing. In the years that followed, reported accidents at the busiest intersection fell sharply.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It compares two towns, describes their approaches, and argues for one.", "It names a device, traces its history, and explains why it is still in use.", "It describes a problem, presents an unconventional fix, and gives the result.", "It states a belief, explains its origin, and then shows why it is mistaken."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The text moves from accidents (problem) to removing signals (solution) to fewer accidents (outcome). Only one town is discussed, no device's history is traced, and no belief is stated and then debunked." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990, but its first images were blurry because its main mirror had been ground to a slightly wrong shape. In 1993, astronauts installed corrective optics during a spacewalk mission. Later servicing missions replaced instruments and batteries, and by 2009 the telescope was more capable than when it launched.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It explains a scientific principle and then gives several examples of it.", "It traces in order how a telescope's flaw was fixed and its tools upgraded.", "It compares the Hubble Space Telescope with a telescope that replaced it.", "It presents a debate about the telescope and then settles that debate."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The text moves through 1990, 1993, and 2009, from the flawed mirror to its repair and later upgrades. It states no general principle, names no replacement telescope, and describes no debate." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Nearly all fish have bodies that stay at the temperature of the surrounding water. The opah, a large fish of the open ocean, keeps its body several degrees warmer than the water around it by circulating blood warmed by the muscles that constantly beat its fins. Opahs appear to use this warmth to stay alert while hunting in cold, deep water.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It presents an exception to the general pattern stated in the first sentence.", "It restates the first sentence's claim about all fish by using a specific example.", "It offers a conclusion drawn from the evidence given in the third sentence.", "It defines a technical term that is used later in the text's final sentence."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The first sentence says nearly all fish match the water's temperature, and the opah is a fish that does not, an exception. It contradicts rather than restates the first claim, the third sentence builds on it rather than supplying evidence for it, and no term is defined." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Sourdough bread rises without commercial yeast. Instead, bakers use a starter, a mixture of flour and water colonized by wild yeasts and bacteria from the environment. The yeasts release carbon dioxide that lifts the dough, while the bacteria produce lactic and acetic acids that give the bread its tangy flavor.\n\nWhich choice best states the main purpose of the text?", options: ["To argue that sourdough has more flavor than bread made with commercial yeast", "To criticize bakers who rely on commercial yeast and ignore wild yeasts", "To describe the history of sourdough starters from ancient times to today", "To explain how a sourdough starter makes bread rise and gives it flavor"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text neutrally explains what a starter is and what its yeasts and bacteria do. It never compares the flavor of sourdough with other breads, criticizes no one, and gives no history." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Supporters of the four-day school week point to lower transportation costs and easier teacher recruitment in the rural districts that have adopted it. Critics contend that longer school days tire young students and that families struggle to find child care on the fifth day. Research on how the schedule affects test scores, meanwhile, has produced mixed results.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It presents objections to a policy whose benefits the text first describes.", "It states the author's own conclusion about the value of the four-day week.", "It provides data showing that the four-day week lowers student achievement.", "It explains why rural districts were the first to adopt the four-day week."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "'Critics contend' attributes the objections to others, answering the benefits that supporters cite in the first sentence. The author takes no position, the second sentence contains no data (research findings come only in the third, and they are mixed), and it says nothing about why rural districts adopted the schedule." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "A comet's tail can look like a single streak of light. In fact, many comets have two tails: a curved one made of dust and a straight, faint one made of electrically charged gas that the solar wind pushes directly away from the Sun. Studying the gas tail, then, can reveal changes in the solar wind itself.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the last sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It repeats the observation that the text presents in its first sentence.", "It offers evidence that challenges the description of the two tails.", "It draws out a research use that follows from the fact described before it.", "It explains why many comets appear to have a single tail made of dust."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "'Then' signals a consequence: because the solar wind shapes the gas tail, the tail can reveal changes in the wind. The sentence does not repeat the first sentence, challenge the two-tail description, or explain why tails look single." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Economists use the term 'opportunity cost' for the value of the best alternative given up when making a choice. A student who spends a Saturday working a six-hour shift at a café, for instance, earns wages but gives up whatever she would otherwise have done with that time, such as studying for an exam that could raise her grade.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It describes a student's choice and then criticizes the decision she made.", "It defines an economic concept and then applies it to a specific situation.", "It contrasts two economic concepts and explains why one is more useful.", "It presents a problem that students face and then proposes a solution."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The first sentence defines opportunity cost, and 'for instance' introduces an application to a student's Saturday. The student's choice is not criticized, only one concept appears, and no solution is proposed." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In August 1883, the volcanic island of Krakatoa, in present-day Indonesia, erupted with such force that the explosion was heard nearly 4,800 kilometers away. The ash it released circled the globe, and for months afterward, sunsets as far away as New York glowed an unusual red. Scientists studying these reports gained some of the first evidence that material in the upper atmosphere could travel around the world.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It gives the date and place of the eruption that the text goes on to discuss.", "It presents a claim about the eruption that scientists later disputed.", "It describes widespread effects that help explain what scientists learned.", "It explains why the eruption could be heard at such a great distance away."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The globe-circling ash and red sunsets are the reports from which scientists learned that upper-atmosphere material travels worldwide. The date and place appear in the first sentence, scientists built on these reports rather than disputing them, and the sound of the eruption is not explained." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The following text is from a short story. Grandpa Lou never threw anything away. His garage held three broken lawn mowers, a canoe with a hole in it, and forty years of newspapers stacked to the ceiling. 'You never know,' he'd say whenever my mother suggested a cleanup. So when my bike chain snapped two miles from home, I knew exactly where to go.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It shows that the narrator's mother disapproves of Lou's hobbies.", "It illustrates the habit that later makes Lou's garage a useful resource.", "It explains why the narrator's bike chain snapped two miles from home.", "It suggests that Lou has recently begun to clean out his garage."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The list of saved junk illustrates that Lou never throws anything away, which is why the narrator heads to his garage for a fix. The mother's view appears in the next sentence and concerns cleanup, the chain's snapping is not explained, and the garage is still full." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "In 1928, Alexander Fleming returned from vacation to find mold growing in one of his bacterial cultures, with a clear ring around it where the bacteria had died. His observation led to the development of penicillin. Stories like Fleming's are often told as tales of luck, but historians of science note that such discoveries depend on researchers who recognize the importance of the unexpected and have the training to pursue it.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the first two sentences in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["They provide evidence that most scientific discoveries result from pure luck.", "They describe an experiment that historians have since shown to be flawed.", "They explain the chemical process by which mold is able to kill bacteria.", "They offer a familiar example that the text then uses to make a broader point."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The Fleming story sets up the text's broader claim that such discoveries depend on prepared researchers, not luck alone. The text argues against the pure-luck view, never calls the work flawed, and does not explain the chemistry." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "In the 1930s, severe drought struck the southern Great Plains after decades in which farmers had plowed up native grasses to plant wheat. Without the deep-rooted grasses to hold it in place, the dry topsoil blew away in enormous dust storms. The storms destroyed crops and forced hundreds of thousands of people to leave the region.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It explains how a combination of causes led to a series of harmful effects.", "It compares farming methods used before and after a severe drought struck.", "It describes a problem and then evaluates several proposed solutions to it.", "It presents two theories about the drought and argues in favor of one of them."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Drought plus plowed-up grasses (causes) led to dust storms, which led to lost crops and migration (effects). No later farming methods, proposed solutions, or competing theories appear." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "It is true that electric cars require more energy and produce more emissions to manufacture than gasoline cars, largely because of their batteries. Some commentators conclude that electric cars are therefore no better for the climate. But analyses of vehicles' full lifetimes find that, in most regions, an electric car makes up for its higher manufacturing emissions within a few years of typical driving and produces lower total emissions over its life.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It lists the advantages of electric cars and then describes their main drawback.", "It grants a point about electric cars, then rejects an inference drawn from it.", "It presents two conflicting analyses of electric cars without taking a position.", "It explains how batteries are made and why their production creates emissions."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "'It is true that' concedes higher manufacturing emissions, and 'But' rejects the conclusion that electric cars are no better for the climate. The drawback comes first, not last; the text clearly takes a position; and battery production is mentioned, not explained." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "For centuries, the ruins at Great Zimbabwe puzzled European visitors, some of whom wrongly credited the stone walls to foreign builders. That debate has since been settled. Archaeological evidence, including pottery, tools, and radiocarbon dates, shows that the city was built between the 11th and 15th centuries by ancestors of the Shona people who still live in the region.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It marks a shift from a past misconception to the evidence that corrects it.", "It introduces the main claim that European visitors made about the ruins.", "It suggests that the question of who built the walls may never be answered.", "It provides the first evidence about when the city was constructed."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The short sentence turns from the visitors' mistaken claims to the archaeological evidence that settled the question. The visitors' claim appears in the first sentence, the second says the debate is settled, and it contains no evidence itself." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Linguists have noticed that the most frequently used verbs in English, such as 'be,' 'have,' and 'go,' are also the most irregular. One explanation is that irregular forms must be memorized one by one, and only very common words are heard often enough to be remembered that way. Rarer irregular verbs, by contrast, tend to become regular over time: few speakers today say 'holp' instead of 'helped.'\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It states a rule, lists its exceptions, and argues that the rule should change.", "It describes a debate, summarizes each side, and declares one side the winner.", "It defines a term, traces its history, and explains how its meaning shifted.", "It notes a pattern, offers an explanation for it, and gives supporting evidence."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text observes that common verbs are irregular (pattern), proposes memorization as the reason (explanation), and cites rare verbs becoming regular (evidence). It argues for no change, presents no two-sided debate, and defines no term." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The following text is from a review of a museum exhibit. The curators have gathered more than two hundred objects, from hand-drawn maps to ship logbooks, yet the exhibit never feels crowded. Each room builds on the last, and the brief wall texts invite visitors to linger rather than hurry. Rarely has an exhibit about navigation been so easy to navigate.\n\nWhich choice best describes the reviewer's overall attitude toward the exhibit?", options: ["Critical of the large number of objects it tries to include", "Neutral, listing its contents without evaluating them", "Admiring of how clearly and thoughtfully it is organized", "Disappointed that its wall texts are short and lack detail"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Phrases such as 'never feels crowded,' 'builds on the last,' and 'so easy to navigate' praise the exhibit's organization. The reviewer says the many objects do not crowd it, clearly evaluates rather than lists, and presents the brief wall texts as a strength." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "The Titanic's builders equipped it with watertight compartments designed to keep the ship afloat even if several of them flooded. Ironically, the confidence these compartments inspired may have contributed to the disaster: believing the ship nearly unsinkable, its operators carried lifeboats for only about half the people on board.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the word \"Ironically\" in the text?", options: ["It signals that the author doubts the compartments were truly watertight.", "It signals that a safety feature had an effect opposite to its purpose.", "It signals a shift from describing the ship to describing its passengers.", "It signals that the builders intended the ship to carry few lifeboats."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The compartments were meant to make the ship safer, yet the confidence they created led to too few lifeboats, an outcome opposite to their purpose. The text does not question whether they were watertight, does not turn to the passengers, and attributes the lifeboat decision to overconfidence, not design intent." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Two explanations have been proposed for why zebras have stripes. The camouflage hypothesis holds that stripes blur a zebra's outline, confusing predators such as lions. The insect hypothesis holds that stripes deter biting flies, which struggle to land on striped surfaces. Field studies support the second: flies approach zebras as often as they approach solid-colored horses but land on zebras far less often.\n\nWhich choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", options: ["It describes a trait of zebras and then explains how that trait changed over time.", "It presents one hypothesis about zebras and then lists several reasons to reject it.", "It compares zebras and horses in order to show that the two share many traits.", "It presents two hypotheses about a trait and then describes evidence favoring one."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The text lays out the camouflage and insect hypotheses and then reports field evidence supporting the insect one. It does not trace change over time, it presents two hypotheses rather than one with a list of objections, and the horse comparison is part of the evidence, not the point." },
  { skillSlug: S.structure, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Why did the Maya abandon many of their great cities in the southern lowlands during the 9th and 10th centuries? Some researchers point to a series of severe droughts recorded in lake sediments. Others emphasize warfare among rival city-states, which intensified in the same period. Still others argue that deforestation and soil loss had already weakened the region's farming. Many scholars now suspect these pressures interacted.\n\nWhich choice best states the main purpose of the text?", options: ["To argue that drought was the main cause of the cities' decline", "To describe daily life in Maya cities before they were abandoned", "To outline a range of proposed explanations for a historical event", "To explain how lake sediments reveal the climates of the past"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The text surveys drought, warfare, and environmental damage as explanations and notes that they may have worked together. It does not single out drought, describe daily life, or explain the sediment method." },

  // ===== Cross-Text Connections (25) =====
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nMany ecologists have argued that wolves reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 triggered a dramatic recovery of streamside willows. By hunting elk and making them wary of lingering near streams, the argument goes, wolves allowed willows that the elk had been eating to grow back.\n\nText 2\n\nIn a long-term experiment, ecologists fenced elk out of willow plots for two decades, yet the willows still grew poorly unless streams were also restored. Years of heavy elk browsing had lowered water tables as beaver dams disappeared, and without that water, reduced browsing alone could not bring the willows back.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the ecologists in Text 2 most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?", options: ["They would argue that less browsing by itself may not explain willow recovery.", "They would argue that wolves did not reduce the number of elk in the park.", "They would agree that wolves were the main cause of the willows' recovery.", "They would argue that elk browsing may have little effect on the growth of willows."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Text 2 found that removing elk was not enough without restored water, so it would question crediting willow recovery to reduced browsing alone. Text 2 never discusses wolves' effect on elk numbers, does not endorse Text 1's view, and treats browsing as one cause of the damage, not as harmless." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nHandwriting notes in class, some researchers argue, leads to better learning than typing. Because handwriting is slower, students cannot copy lectures word for word and must summarize ideas in their own words, which helps them remember the material.\n\nText 2\n\nLater attempts to repeat the original handwriting study produced mixed results. In several, students who typed remembered lecture content about as well as those who wrote by hand, suggesting that the advantage, if it exists, may be smaller than first reported.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize the view in Text 1?", options: ["It correctly identifies why handwriting improves students' memory of lectures.", "It may rely on studies in which students were not allowed to take any notes.", "It may overstate a benefit that other studies have not consistently found.", "It ignores evidence that typing is much better than handwriting for learning."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Text 2 reports mixed replications and says any advantage may be smaller than reported, so it would see Text 1's claim as possibly overstated. Text 2 doubts rather than confirms the benefit, never says students took no notes, and does not claim typing is better." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nSome astronomers have proposed that an undiscovered planet, several times Earth's mass, orbits far beyond Neptune. They note that a group of distant icy objects have orbits that cluster in the same direction, a pattern they say the gravity of a large planet would explain.\n\nText 2\n\nOther astronomers suggest that the clustering may be an illusion. Surveys can detect distant objects only in certain parts of the sky and at certain times of year, so the objects found so far may not represent the full population.\n\nWhich choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?", options: ["It offers new evidence that confirms the proposal made in Text 1.", "It explains how the planet described in Text 1 was formed.", "It agrees with Text 1 but suggests the planet is smaller.", "It questions the evidence on which Text 1's proposal is based."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Text 1's proposal rests on the clustering of orbits, and Text 2 suggests that clustering may be an artifact of where surveys look. Text 2 challenges rather than confirms the proposal and says nothing about the planet's formation or size." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nStudies of ancient DNA show that dogs were domesticated from wolves at least 15,000 years ago, well before humans began farming. This timing suggests that dogs first lived alongside hunter-gatherers.\n\nText 2\n\nArchaeologist Angela Perri has noted that dogs may have been especially valuable to hunter-gatherers as hunting partners. Burials in which dogs were placed carefully beside humans, some more than 14,000 years old, suggest how much these animals mattered to the people who kept them.\n\nBased on the texts, how does the information in Text 2 relate to the claim in Text 1 that dogs first lived with hunter-gatherers?", options: ["It challenges the claim by suggesting dogs may have been domesticated after farming.", "It builds on the claim by indicating what dogs may have done for those groups.", "It repeats the claim without adding new information about ancient dogs.", "It is unrelated to the claim because it focuses only on burial practices."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Text 2 accepts that dogs lived with hunter-gatherers and adds what they may have done for them: hunting and companionship valued enough to be buried with people. It does not dispute the timing, it adds new information, and the burials are used to show dogs' importance, not as a separate topic." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nIn 2023, average test scores in a school district's elementary schools rose four points after the district extended the school day by thirty minutes. District officials have called the increase proof that the longer day is working and plan to expand it to middle schools.\n\nText 2\n\nThe district's four-point gain is real, but it is roughly the same increase that neighboring districts without longer days also saw that year. Before expanding the program, officials should determine whether the longer day, rather than a statewide trend, produced the gain.\n\nWhich choice best describes a difference in how the officials in Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view the four-point increase?", options: ["The officials credit the new schedule, while Text 2's author suspects another cause.", "The officials say the scores rose, while Text 2's author says the scores actually fell.", "The officials see it as small, while Text 2's author considers it an unusually large gain.", "The officials doubt its accuracy, while Text 2's author accepts the reported figures."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Both accept the gain as real; they differ on its cause, since the officials attribute it to the longer day and Text 2 points to a statewide trend. Text 2 says the gain is real, calls it typical rather than large, and the officials do not doubt the figures." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nBecause octopuses solve puzzles, use tools, and learn by watching one another, some researchers argue that they possess a form of intelligence comparable to that of many mammals.\n\nText 2\n\nOctopus intelligence evolved independently of vertebrate intelligence, and much of an octopus's nervous system lies in its arms, which can respond to touch without input from the brain. Comparing octopuses to mammals on a single scale of intelligence may therefore obscure how differently their minds are organized.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the researchers in Text 1?", options: ["By arguing that octopuses cannot actually solve puzzles or use any tools", "By agreeing that octopus and mammal intelligence differ very little overall", "By suggesting that, compared with mammals, octopuses may learn faster by watching others", "By cautioning that comparing octopuses to mammals may overlook key differences"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Text 2 objects to placing octopuses and mammals on a single scale, because octopus minds are organized so differently. It does not deny octopuses' abilities, does not endorse the comparison, and makes no claim about learning speed." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nHistorian Emma Clarke argues that the printing press spread quickly across Europe in the late 1400s mainly because printed books were far cheaper than hand-copied manuscripts.\n\nText 2\n\nCost alone cannot explain the press's rapid spread, argues historian Paul Mendez. He notes that printers clustered in trading cities, where merchants' networks could distribute books widely, and that demand from universities and churches gave early printers reliable customers.\n\nBased on the texts, Clarke and Mendez would most likely agree that the printing press", options: ["spread quickly mainly because printed books cost so little.", "moved rapidly through Europe during the late 1400s.", "owed its rapid spread mainly to merchants' trade networks.", "spread across Europe slowly until universities bought books."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Both historians take the press's rapid spread as given and disagree only about why. Low cost as the main reason is Clarke's view, which Mendez says is insufficient; Mendez cites trade networks as one factor among several; and neither describes a slow spread." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nPaleontologists who studied fossilized pigment structures in the feathers of the small dinosaur Anchiornis have reconstructed its colors: a gray body, black-and-white wings, and a reddish crest. These findings show that we can now know exactly what some dinosaurs looked like.\n\nText 2\n\nPigment structures in fossils can change shape as they decay and as rock forms around them. Researchers studying Anchiornis took such changes into account, but their color reconstruction should be viewed as a well-supported estimate rather than a certainty.\n\nWhich choice best describes a difference in how the authors view the color reconstruction of Anchiornis?", options: ["Text 1 regards it as a strong but uncertain estimate, whereas Text 2 treats it as certain.", "Text 1 focuses on its wing colors, whereas Text 2 focuses mainly on the crest.", "Text 1 treats it as certain, whereas Text 2 sees it as a strong but uncertain estimate.", "Text 1 accepts its methods, whereas Text 2 claims pigment structures never survive."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Text 1 says we can know 'exactly' what the dinosaur looked like; Text 2 calls the result 'a well-supported estimate rather than a certainty.' The attitudes are reversed in one choice, Text 2 never singles out the crest, and Text 2 says pigment structures change, not that they never survive." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nGreen roofs, which are covered with soil and plants, can reduce the energy a building needs for cooling by insulating it and shading its surface. Cities such as Toronto now require green roofs on many new buildings.\n\nText 2\n\nThe cooling benefit of a green roof depends on the building. For tall buildings, the roof makes up a small share of the total surface exposed to the sun, so a green roof reduces cooling needs mainly on the top floors.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1 about green roofs and cooling?", options: ["By noting that the cooling effect is weaker for some kinds of buildings", "By arguing that green roofs increase the energy used for cooling", "By agreeing that green roofs cool every floor of every building equally well", "By noting that green roofs grow poorly on some kinds of tall buildings"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Text 2 accepts that green roofs cool buildings but says the benefit is smaller for tall buildings, a qualification of Text 1. It never says green roofs raise energy use, it says the benefit is concentrated on top floors, and it raises no problem with plants growing on tall buildings." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nAnalyses of ice cores from Greenland show that lead pollution rose during the centuries of Roman expansion and fell during periods of plague and war. Researchers link the pattern to Roman silver mining, which released lead into the air as ore was smelted.\n\nText 2\n\nRecords of Roman coinage show that the silver content of coins, and thus the output of silver mines, rose and fell during the same centuries. Economic historians use these records to track the health of the Roman economy.\n\nBased on the texts, how are the two kinds of evidence related?", options: ["Text 2's records show a mining output pattern that contradicts the ice core data in Text 1.", "The two records point to the same rise and fall in Roman mining output.", "Each suggests that lead pollution caused the plagues and wars described in Text 1.", "Text 1's ice cores show that Roman coins contained more lead than silver."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Ice core lead and coin silver both rose and fell over the same centuries, independently pointing to the same pattern in mining output. The coin records agree with rather than contradict the ice cores, the plagues and wars reduced pollution rather than resulting from it, and the ice cores say nothing about coins' composition." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nTraditional museums often keep visitors at a distance from objects, placing them behind glass with brief labels. Museum director Nina Simon has argued that museums should instead invite visitors to participate, for example by sharing their own stories or helping to create exhibits.\n\nText 2\n\nHands-on and participatory exhibits can draw new audiences, but curators must also protect fragile objects. Many museums now balance these goals by letting visitors handle replicas while keeping originals behind glass.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Simon's argument?", options: ["By rejecting it because visitors show little interest in participating at all", "By accepting its value while arguing that visitors should handle fragile objects", "By claiming that brief labels are the best way to engage new visitors", "By accepting its value while noting that fragile objects need safeguarding"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Text 2 agrees that participation draws new audiences but adds that fragile objects must be protected, as with replicas. It does not reject participation, keeps originals behind glass, and does not praise brief labels." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nThe theory of island dwarfism holds that large animals isolated on islands tend to evolve smaller bodies over many generations, because limited food favors individuals that need less of it.\n\nText 2\n\nOn the Mediterranean island of Sicily, fossils show that elephants descended from a mainland species about 4 meters tall eventually shrank to about 1 meter at the shoulder. Researchers estimate that the change took place gradually, over many generations, after the elephants reached the island.\n\nBased on the texts, how does the example in Text 2 relate to the theory in Text 1?", options: ["It provides a case that contradicts the theory's main prediction.", "It shows that the theory applies to mainland animals as well.", "It provides a case that is consistent with the theory's prediction.", "It shows that island dwarfism occurs within a few generations."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Large elephants isolated on Sicily shrank over many generations, just as the theory predicts. The case supports rather than contradicts the theory, it involves island animals, and the change took many generations, not a few." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nWhen nutritionists call a food 'processed,' they often mean any food altered from its natural state, a definition that includes frozen vegetables and pasteurized milk. By that standard, most foods people eat are processed, and processing itself is not harmful.\n\nText 2\n\nResearchers who warn about processed food usually mean 'ultra-processed' products: industrial formulations such as packaged snacks and soft drinks made largely from refined ingredients and additives. Diets high in these products have been linked to weight gain.\n\nWhich choice best explains why the texts might appear to disagree about processed food?", options: ["They use the term 'processed' for different groups of foods.", "They disagree about whether frozen vegetables are processed foods.", "They rely on the same studies but reach opposite conclusions.", "They disagree about whether diet has any effect on body weight."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Text 1 uses 'processed' broadly, while Text 2's warnings concern a narrower category, ultra-processed products, so the apparent conflict is about definitions. Text 2 never discusses frozen vegetables, neither text cites shared studies, and Text 1 says nothing about weight." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nWhen one country introduced a tax on sugary drinks in 2018, some economists predicted that consumers would simply switch to untaxed sweets, leaving total sugar consumption unchanged.\n\nText 2\n\nSupermarket purchase data from the country show that, in the two years after the tax took effect, sales of taxed drinks fell by a fifth, sales of untaxed sweets rose only slightly, and total sugar purchased per household declined.\n\nBased on the texts, how does the information in Text 2 bear on the prediction in Text 1?", options: ["It confirms the prediction, since purchases of sugary sweets rose slightly.", "It suggests the prediction was wrong, since total sugar purchases fell.", "It suggests the prediction was right, since total drink purchases dropped.", "It is unrelated to the prediction, which concerned taxed drinks only."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The economists predicted unchanged total sugar consumption, but Text 2 reports that total sugar purchases declined. A slight rise in sweets is far from full substitution, a drop in drink sales alone does not support the prediction, and the prediction was about switching to sweets and total sugar, not drinks only." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nSupporters of a proposed protected bike lane on Elm Street argue that it would reduce injuries, pointing out that similar lanes cut cyclist injuries by about a third in nearby cities.\n\nText 2\n\nOwners of shops on Elm Street oppose the lane because it would remove forty parking spaces. They do not dispute its safety benefits, but they worry that customers who drive will shop elsewhere.\n\nWhich choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?", options: ["Text 2 accepts the safety claim in Text 1 but raises another concern.", "Text 2 offers evidence that the lane would not improve cyclist safety.", "Text 2 agrees with Text 1 that the bike lane should be built right away.", "Text 2 endorses the lane's goal but disputes its safety benefits."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The shop owners 'do not dispute its safety benefits' but oppose the lane over lost parking. They offer no evidence about injuries, they oppose building the lane, and they accept rather than dispute the safety benefits." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nA 2019 survey found that people who owned dogs reported exercising more than people who did not. The survey's authors concluded that getting a dog leads people to become more active.\n\nText 2\n\nThe dog survey compared owners with non-owners at a single point in time. People who already enjoy walking and exercise may be more likely to get a dog in the first place, so the survey cannot show that owning a dog changed anyone's habits.\n\nBased on the texts, the author of Text 2 would most likely criticize the conclusion in Text 1 on the grounds that", options: ["the dog owners actually exercise less than they reported.", "researchers should have compared different breeds of dogs.", "people without dogs were not included in the survey at all.", "the survey's design leaves unclear what caused the difference."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Text 2 argues that a one-time comparison cannot tell whether dogs made people active or active people got dogs. It does not question the reported exercise levels, never mentions breeds, and notes that non-owners were included." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nThe Silk Road was not a single road but a web of routes linking China with Central Asia, Persia, and the Mediterranean. Silk, spices, and precious metals moved along it, often changing hands many times before reaching their final buyers.\n\nText 2\n\nAlong with goods, the Silk Road carried ideas. Buddhism spread from India into China partly through merchants and monks who traveled its routes, and papermaking techniques eventually moved westward toward the Islamic world.\n\nWhich choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?", options: ["Text 2 disputes Text 1's description of the Silk Road's routes.", "Text 2 argues that trade in goods on the Silk Road was minor.", "Text 2 discusses a kind of exchange that Text 1 does not address.", "Text 2 repeats Text 1's claims about the goods that were bought and sold."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Text 1 covers trade in goods, and Text 2 adds the exchange of ideas and techniques ('Along with goods'). Text 2 does not dispute the routes, downplay trade in goods, or repeat Text 1's list of goods." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nFrom a climate researcher: Our models project that sea levels along this coast will rise between 0.3 and 1.1 meters by 2100. The wide range reflects real uncertainty about how quickly polar ice will melt, and we caution against treating any single figure as a forecast.\n\nText 2\n\nFrom a city planner: Our city cannot wait for scientists to narrow their estimates. Because seawalls take decades to build, we are designing new infrastructure to withstand a rise of 1 meter, near the upper end of current projections.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the planner in Text 2 most likely respond to the researcher's caution in Text 1?", options: ["By arguing that sea levels will certainly rise by more than 1.1 meters", "By accepting the uncertainty but delaying construction until it narrows", "By rejecting the researcher's estimates as too uncertain for planning", "By accepting the uncertainty but planning for a high estimate anyway"], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The planner accepts that estimates are uncertain ('cannot wait for scientists to narrow their estimates') and designs for a rise near the top of the range. The planner claims no certainty, refuses to wait, and uses the projections rather than rejecting them." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nBefore the 1960s, most geologists believed that the continents were fixed in place. Alfred Wegener's earlier proposal that continents drift had been widely dismissed, partly because he could not explain what force would move them.\n\nText 2\n\nMaps of the seafloor made in the 1950s and 1960s revealed long underwater ridges where new crust forms and spreads outward. This seafloor spreading supplied the mechanism that Wegener lacked, and the theory of plate tectonics soon gained broad acceptance.\n\nBased on the texts, how does the information in Text 2 relate to the objection described in Text 1?", options: ["It resolves the problem by showing how continents could move.", "It shows that Wegener's critics were right that continents are fixed.", "It explains why seafloor maps were not available to Wegener's critics.", "It suggests that Wegener himself first described seafloor spreading."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Text 1 says Wegener was dismissed because he lacked a force to move continents, and Text 2 says seafloor spreading 'supplied the mechanism that Wegener lacked.' Text 2 supports drift, does not explain when maps became available, and credits the discovery to later mapping, not Wegener." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nMultitasking always harms performance. When people switch between tasks, they lose time and make more errors than when they complete the tasks one at a time.\n\nText 2\n\nSwitching between two demanding tasks, such as writing an email while following a lecture, reliably slows people down. But when one task is highly practiced and nearly automatic, such as walking, people can often combine it with another task at little cost.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?", options: ["By rejecting it entirely, since switching tasks never slows people down", "By accepting it fully, since every task combination causes errors", "By agreeing it holds for demanding tasks but not for all combinations", "By agreeing it holds for practiced tasks but not for demanding ones"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Text 2 accepts that demanding tasks interfere but identifies an exception (an automatic task plus another), narrowing Text 1's 'always.' It agrees that switching slows people in some cases, it does not accept the claim fully, and it treats practiced tasks, not demanding ones, as the exception." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nMany readers assume that Emily Dickinson was a recluse who wrote only for herself. She rarely left her family home in Amherst, and fewer than a dozen of her nearly 1,800 poems were published in her lifetime.\n\nText 2\n\nDickinson's letters complicate the image of an isolated poet. She corresponded with dozens of people and often enclosed poems in her letters, sharing her work with friends, family, and editors throughout her life.\n\nBased on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?", options: ["By agreeing that Dickinson wrote her poems only for her own use", "By arguing that Dickinson shared her poems more widely than it suggests", "By suggesting that most of Dickinson's poems were published in her life", "By claiming that Dickinson traveled widely beyond her home in Amherst"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Text 2 shows Dickinson sending poems to dozens of correspondents, which challenges the idea that she wrote only for herself. It disputes rather than accepts the assumption, does not contradict the publication figure, and concerns letters, not travel." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nThe purpose of the Nazca Lines, giant figures and lines etched into the Peruvian desert, remains debated. Researcher Maria Reiche proposed that many of the lines served as an astronomical calendar, pointing to where the Sun rose on important dates.\n\nText 2\n\nLater surveys found that the lines do not align with celestial events any more often than chance would predict. Some researchers now suggest that the lines marked paths for ritual processions, perhaps connected to water, though this idea is also difficult to test.\n\nWhich choice best describes the relationship between the texts?", options: ["Text 2 casts doubt on Text 1's proposal and offers a tentative alternative.", "Text 2 confirms Text 1's proposal using new, more careful astronomical surveys.", "Text 2 argues that the lines' purpose has now been settled with certainty.", "Text 2 agrees with Text 1's proposal and questions only the lines' dating."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "Text 2 reports that alignments occur no more often than chance, undermining the calendar idea, and offers a ritual-path idea that it admits is hard to test. It does not confirm Reiche's view, treats the question as unsettled, and never discusses dating." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nThe city's new glass-walled library is a triumph. Light floods its reading rooms, and its open design invites visitors to wander from the children's area to the rooftop garden.\n\nText 2\n\nThe new library is striking to look at, but its glass walls make the reading rooms uncomfortably hot by midafternoon, and the open floor plan carries noise from the café into the study areas.\n\nWhich choice best describes a difference in the authors' attitudes toward the library?", options: ["Text 1 criticizes its design, while Text 2 praises it without any reservation.", "Text 1 focuses on its cost, while Text 2 focuses mainly on its location.", "Text 1 is neutral about the building, while Text 2 is enthusiastic about it.", "Text 1 praises it without reservation, while Text 2 points out practical flaws."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Text 1 calls the library 'a triumph,' while Text 2 grants its looks but criticizes heat and noise. The attitudes are reversed in one choice, neither text discusses cost or location, and Text 1 is enthusiastic rather than neutral." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Text 1\n\nMany migratory birds can find their way on cloudy nights, and some biologists argue that a light-sensitive protein in their eyes lets them sense Earth's magnetic field, giving them a built-in compass.\n\nText 2\n\nOther biologists have pointed to tiny iron-rich structures found in some birds' beaks as the source of magnetic sensing. Birds, in this view, detect the magnetic field through nerves in the beak rather than through their eyes.\n\nBased on the texts, the biologists in Text 1 and Text 2 would most likely agree that", options: ["a protein in birds' eyes senses the magnetic field.", "iron in birds' beaks makes navigation possible.", "birds can sense Earth's magnetic field.", "birds sense Earth's field only through their beaks."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Both groups accept that birds sense the magnetic field and disagree only about the sensing organ. The eye protein is Text 1's view, the beak structures are Text 2's, and Text 1 would reject the view that birds sense the field only through their beaks." },
  { skillSlug: S.cross, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Text 1\n\nFour-day workweeks have boosted employee well-being in several trials. In a 2022 program involving about 60 British companies, most firms chose to keep the shorter schedule after the six-month trial ended.\n\nText 2\n\nThe British four-day-week trial relied on companies that volunteered, which may have been especially well suited to shorter schedules. Results from a broader range of industries are needed before the findings can be generalized.\n\nBased on the texts, the authors of both texts would most likely agree that the British trial", options: ["suggests that four-day weeks would work well in most industries.", "yielded results that many participating companies viewed favorably.", "included a representative sample of all British companies.", "caused employee well-being to decline in most of the companies."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Text 2 does not dispute that most participating firms kept the schedule; it questions only whether the results generalize. Text 2 rejects the idea that the trial shows the schedule would suit most industries or that the sample was representative, and neither text reports declining well-being." },

  // ===== Rhetorical Synthesis (11) =====
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Mae Jemison is an American engineer and physician.\n• In 1992, she flew aboard the space shuttle Endeavour.\n• She was the first African American woman to travel to space.\n• Before joining NASA, she worked as a doctor in the Peace Corps.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize Jemison's historic achievement. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Mae Jemison is an American woman who works as both an engineer and a physician.", "In 1992, Mae Jemison became the first African American woman to travel to space.", "Before she would travel to space, Mae Jemison worked as a doctor in the Peace Corps.", "Mae Jemison, an African American engineer, flew aboard the shuttle Endeavour in 1992."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Only the statement that she was the first African American woman in space conveys what made her achievement historic. The others accurately report her profession, Peace Corps service, or shuttle, but none highlights why the flight was historic." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Hadrian's Wall was built across northern Britain beginning in 122 CE.\n• Roman soldiers built it to mark and defend the empire's frontier.\n• The Great Wall of China was built and rebuilt over many centuries.\n• Chinese dynasties built it to defend their northern frontier.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two walls. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Both walls were built by Roman soldiers to defend the empire's frontier.", "Roman soldiers built Hadrian's Wall across northern Britain beginning in 122 CE.", "The Great Wall of China was built and rebuilt by Chinese dynasties over centuries.", "Both Hadrian's Wall and the Great Wall of China were built to defend a frontier."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "The shared purpose, defending a frontier, is the similarity the notes support. The claim that Roman soldiers built both walls is false for the Great Wall, which Chinese dynasties built, and the other two choices describe only one wall each." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Neuroscientists studied how sleep affects memory.\n• Participants learned a list of word pairs in the evening.\n• Half of them then slept normally; the other half stayed awake all night.\n• After two nights of recovery sleep, the group that had slept recalled more word pairs.\n\nThe student wants to present the study's aim and method to an audience unfamiliar with the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Participants who slept after learning word pairs later recalled more of them than those who stayed awake.", "After two nights of recovery sleep, participants were tested on the word pairs they had learned earlier.", "To study sleep and memory, researchers had people learn word pairs and then sleep or stay up all night.", "In this study, researchers found that sleeping after learning improves memory more than staying awake."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The goal is the study's aim and method, so the answer must say what was studied and what participants did. Two choices report the result instead, and the one about recovery sleep describes only the final test without the aim or the key comparison." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is located in Norway's Arctic.\n• It stores duplicate samples of seeds from gene banks around the world.\n• The seeds serve as a backup in case collections are lost to war or natural disasters.\n• In 2015, seeds were withdrawn to replace samples from a gene bank in Syria damaged by war.\n\nThe student wants to explain the purpose of the seed vault. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["By safeguarding duplicate seeds, the Svalbard vault lets gene banks recover from losses.", "Located in Norway's Arctic, the Svalbard vault stores seeds from gene banks around the world.", "In 2015, seeds were withdrawn from the Svalbard vault to replace samples from a Syrian gene bank.", "The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, in Norway's Arctic, holds duplicate samples of many kinds of seeds."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The purpose is to serve as a backup so lost collections can be replaced, which the answer states directly. The others give the vault's location, contents, or a single withdrawal without stating why the vault exists." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'hard', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Mount Everest's summit is 8,849 meters above sea level.\n• Mauna Kea, in Hawaii, rises 4,207 meters above sea level.\n• Mauna Kea's base lies about 6,000 meters below the ocean surface.\n• Measured from base to summit, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 meters tall.\n\nThe student wants to explain why Mauna Kea can be considered taller than Mount Everest despite its lower summit. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Though its summit is lower than Everest's, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 meters tall from its underwater base.", "At 8,849 meters above sea level, Mount Everest's summit is far higher than the summit of Mauna Kea.", "Mauna Kea rises 4,207 meters above sea level, and its base lies about 6,000 meters below the ocean surface.", "Though Mauna Kea's base lies 6,000 meters underwater, its summit is far lower than Everest's."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The goal requires acknowledging the lower summit and explaining the base-to-summit height that exceeds Everest's. Two choices accurately compare summits but never explain the other measure, and the one about Mauna Kea's base gives the pieces without connecting them to Everest." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• In 2012, the Curiosity rover landed in Gale Crater on Mars.\n• It found rocks formed from sediment deposited in an ancient lake.\n• The lake water appears to have been neither very acidic nor very salty.\n• Scientists concluded that the lake could once have supported microbial life.\n\nThe student wants to present Curiosity's finding and explain its significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["The Curiosity rover landed on Mars in 2012 to search Gale Crater for signs of microbial life.", "Rocks in Gale Crater formed from sediment that was deposited in an ancient lake there.", "Curiosity found rocks from an ancient lake whose water appears to have been fairly mild.", "Curiosity detected signs of an ancient lake whose mild water might have sustained microbes."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "A two-part goal needs the finding (an ancient lake) and its significance (it could have supported life). The landing statement reports no finding and adds a purpose the notes never state, the sediment statement gives the finding alone, and the water-chemistry statement omits why it matters." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'hard', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• The Arctic tern migrates between the Arctic and the Antarctic each year.\n• Its round trip can exceed 70,000 kilometers.\n• The bar-tailed godwit migrates from Alaska to New Zealand.\n• It can fly about 11,000 kilometers nonstop, without feeding or resting.\n\nThe student wants to contrast the two birds' migrations. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Arctic terns fly pole to pole, while godwits also fly thousands of kilometers.", "The tern's migration spans pole to pole, while the godwit's stands out for its nonstop flight.", "The bar-tailed godwit can fly about 11,000 kilometers from Alaska to New Zealand nonstop.", "The Arctic tern migrates between the Arctic and Antarctic, a round trip of over 70,000 kilometers."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "A contrast must mention both birds and state a difference, here range versus nonstop flight. The 'also travels' statement turns the contrast into a similarity, and the other two each describe only one bird." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Researchers placed underwater microphones along a coral reef in Indonesia.\n• Healthy reefs are noisy with the sounds of fish and shrimp.\n• Damaged reefs are much quieter.\n• The researchers want to use sound recordings to track whether reef restoration is working.\n\nThe student wants to explain the purpose of the research. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Researchers placed underwater microphones off the coast of Indonesia, recording fish and shrimp sounds.", "A healthy coral reef is noisy with fish and shrimp, while a damaged reef is much quieter.", "The researchers are recording reef sounds to check if efforts to restore reefs are succeeding.", "Restoration has made damaged reefs in Indonesia noisier, as the recordings clearly show."], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The last note gives the purpose: using sound to track whether restoration works. The microphone statement describes the method, the healthy-versus-damaged contrast gives background, and the claim that restoration has already made reefs noisier is not in the notes; finding that out is the goal of the research." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'hard', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• A study tracked about 2,000 adults over ten years.\n• Adults who walked at least 7,000 steps a day had a lower risk of death during the study than those who walked fewer.\n• The benefit leveled off above about 10,000 steps a day.\n• The study was observational, so it cannot prove that walking caused the lower risk.\n\nThe student wants to summarize the study's main finding while acknowledging its limitation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Walking at least 7,000 steps a day lowered adults' risk of death, and walking more always added benefits.", "Walking at least 7,000 steps a day was linked to lower risk of death, though causation is unproven.", "Walking at least 7,000 steps a day lowered risk of death, though benefits kept rising past 10,000 steps.", "The study, which tracked 2,000 adults over ten years, found that walking 10,000 steps a day prevents death."], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The answer reports the association and notes that an observational study cannot prove causation. The others distort the notes: the benefit leveled off rather than always growing, the notes say benefits leveled off above 10,000 steps, and nothing prevents death." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'medium', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Braille is a writing system used by people who are blind or have low vision.\n• Each Braille character is formed from a cell of up to six raised dots.\n• Different arrangements of the dots represent letters, numbers, and punctuation.\n• Louis Braille developed the system in France in the 1820s.\n\nThe student wants to explain how Braille represents information. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Braille, a system of raised letters for people who are blind, was developed in the 1820s.", "Developed in France in the 1820s, Braille represents text for blind and low-vision readers.", "Louis Braille, who developed the system, was working in France during the 1820s.", "In Braille, patterns of up to six raised dots stand for letters, numerals, and other symbols."], correctAnswer: 3, explanation: "Only the statement about dot patterns standing for letters, numerals, and other symbols explains how Braille represents information. Braille uses raised dots, not raised letters, and the other choices describe only who uses Braille and when and where it was developed." },
  { skillSlug: S.synth, difficulty: 'hard', question: "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Traditional vaccines often use weakened or inactivated viruses to train the immune system.\n• Growing and inactivating viruses can take months.\n• mRNA vaccines instead give cells instructions to make a harmless viral protein.\n• mRNA vaccines can be designed within days once a virus's genetic sequence is known.\n\nThe student wants to contrast how quickly the two types of vaccines can be developed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", options: ["Traditional vaccines can take months to make, while mRNA vaccines can be designed in days.", "mRNA vaccines give cells protein instructions, while traditional ones use weakened viruses.", "mRNA vaccines can be designed to give cells instructions for making a harmless viral protein.", "Weakened or inactivated viruses used in traditional vaccines can take months to grow."], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The contrast in development time requires both types and both time frames: months versus days. The statement about weakened viruses and instructions contrasts how the vaccines work, not how fast they are developed, and the other two each describe only one type of vaccine." },

  // ===== Transitions (3) =====
  { skillSlug: S.trans, difficulty: 'medium', question: "The ancient Greek astronomer Aristarchus proposed that Earth orbits the Sun. His idea, however, found few supporters in his own time. ______ it was not until the 16th century, when Nicolaus Copernicus developed a detailed Sun-centered model, that the idea gained serious attention.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", options: ["Similarly,", "For example,", "Indeed,", "In contrast,"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The last sentence reinforces the claim that the idea had few supporters by showing how long it was ignored, so an emphasizing transition fits. It is not a parallel case, not an example, and not a contrast." },
  { skillSlug: S.trans, difficulty: 'medium', question: "Proponents of high-speed rail argue that it can replace many short flights, cutting travel emissions. ______ building new rail lines is expensive and can take decades. Still, in countries such as France and Spain, high-speed rail has already captured most of the travel market on several routes where it competes with airlines.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", options: ["Therefore,", "Similarly,", "Admittedly,", "For instance,"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "The sentence concedes a drawback before 'Still' returns to the argument, so a concessive transition fits. The cost is not a result of the proponents' claim, not a parallel point, and not an example of it." },
  { skillSlug: S.trans, difficulty: 'hard', question: "Beavers build dams that slow the flow of streams and spread water across floodplains. ______ areas with active beaver dams often stay green during droughts and can act as firebreaks, a pattern researchers observed after major wildfires in the western United States.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", options: ["Nevertheless,", "As a result,", "Meanwhile,", "In contrast,"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Green, fire-resistant areas follow from the water that dams spread, so the second sentence states a result. Nothing is being contrasted or conceded, and 'meanwhile' signals a simultaneous but separate development rather than a consequence." },
]
